{
 "lead": "The ratio test in <a href=\"#comparison-integral-ratio-and-root-tests\">3.3</a> often returns 1 and leaves a positive-term series undecided. Raabe's test breaks that tie by measuring how far the ratio aₙ/aₙ₊₁ sits from 1: it multiplies the gap by n and compares the limit with 1. The logarithmic test does the same comparison on a log scale, reading the growth of 1/aₙ as a power of n. Both are refinements, not replacements. Only a few universities teach these tests; they appear in advanced calculus syllabi.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#infinite-series-of-real-numbers\">3.2</a> and <a href=\"#comparison-integral-ratio-and-root-tests\">3.3</a>; factorial and product notation.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Raabe's test: measuring the gap",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The ratio test in <a href=\"#comparison-integral-ratio-and-root-tests\">3.3</a> looks at ρ = lim aₙ₊₁/aₙ. If ρ = 1, it gives no answer. The gap ρ − 1 is too small. Raabe's test multiplies that gap by n, which is large enough to expose the hidden power of n."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Rₙ = n(aₙ/aₙ₊₁ − 1) → L",
     "where": "aₙ > 0 is the nth term; aₙ₊₁ is the next term; Rₙ is the Raabe expression; L is its limit."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "If aₙ behaves like C/n^p, then aₙ/aₙ₊₁ = (1+1/n)^p = 1 + p/n + smaller terms. So Rₙ → p. Since Σ1/n^p converges exactly when p > 1, the threshold is L = 1. This is why the test is a refinement, not a replacement."
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Check aₙ > 0 and ratio limit 1.",
      "Form aₙ/aₙ₊₁.",
      "Subtract 1 and multiply by n.",
      "Take the limit L.",
      "L > 1: converge; L < 1: diverge; L = 1: no conclusion."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "The logarithmic test",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The logarithmic test reads the same exponent from a logarithm. Let aₙ > 0 and suppose (log(1/aₙ))/(log n) → L. Here log is natural logarithm. Then Σaₙ converges for L > 1, diverges for L < 1, and is undecided for L = 1."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "(log(1/aₙ))/(log n) → L",
     "where": "aₙ is the nth positive term; n is the index; log is natural logarithm; L is the limiting exponent."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Why: if aₙ ≈ C/n^L, then log(1/aₙ) = L log n − log C. Divide by log n: L − (log C)/(log n) → L. The test is handy when log aₙ is a sum, such as a factorial product."
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "L > 1: terms shrink faster than 1/n; converge.",
      "L < 1: terms shrink slower than 1/n; diverge.",
      "L = 1: no conclusion; use comparison or integral test."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Use Raabe's reciprocal aₙ/aₙ₊₁, never aₙ₊₁/aₙ. Both tests need positive terms. They refine <a href=\"#comparison-integral-ratio-and-root-tests\">3.3</a>; for signed series study Σ|aₙ| as in <a href=\"#absolute-and-conditional-convergence\">3.5</a>. The commonest slip is writing the ratio upside down: it turns a divergent example into a false convergent one."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
